<?xml version="1.0" ?>
<!--
  warehouse_models_person.world — 6 m × 6 m enclosed room with 5 person
  figures, sized to match the TurtleBot3 LDS-01 LiDAR.

  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  Why 6×6 (not 6×10 like the original)?
  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  TurtleBot3's default LDS-01 has a max effective range of ~3.5 m.
  In the previous 6×10 layout, the centre of the room sat 5 m from
  the north and south walls, so a robot anywhere near the middle
  saw only the two parallel east/west walls — a classic infinite
  corridor that left scan-matching translationally ambiguous along
  the long axis. Coupled with chassis pitch on collisions (which
  briefly tilts the LiDAR plane and rasterizes the floor as fake
  obstacles), this produced the "broken occupancy patches in the
  middle" + "duplicate person ghosts" we observed in RViz.

  6 m × 6 m fixes this geometrically: the centre of the room is
  exactly 3 m from every wall, so the bot always has at least three
  sides within LDS range plus visible corners — scan matching is
  now well-conditioned everywhere.

  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  Geometry
  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  Inner clearance: X ∈ [-3, 3], Y ∈ [-3, 3] (6 m × 6 m).
  Walls: thickness 0.2 m, height 2.5 m, outer faces at ±3.1 m.

  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  Person layout
  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  Five `person_standing` instances. Corner figures sit 1.0 m off
  every nearby wall, leaving room for Nav2's inflation layer to
  produce reachable goals next to each person. Each corner person's
  yaw points toward the room centre so the figure is detectable
  from any approach angle.

    Position (x, y)   Yaw (rad)   Faces        Notes
    ────────────────  ──────────  ───────────  ──────────────────
    ( 2.0,  2.0)      -2.3562     toward (0,0) NE corner (-3π/4)
    (-2.0,  2.0)      -0.7854     toward (0,0) NW corner (-π/4)
    (-2.0, -2.0)       0.7854     toward (0,0) SW corner ( π/4)
    ( 2.0, -2.0)       2.3562     toward (0,0) SE corner ( 3π/4)
    ( 0.0,  0.0)       0          +X            Centre

  Inter-person distance is at least 2.83 m (centre↔corner) and
  4.0 m (corner↔corner along an axis), comfortably above the
  semantic memory `merge_distance` of 1.5 m, so legitimate persons
  cannot be accidentally merged.

  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  Robot spawn
  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  Recommended (set automatically when this world is selected via
  the `world:=warehouse_models_person` alias): (-2.5, 0.0), facing +X.
  Rationale:
    * 0.5 m off the west wall   → scan visible against W wall.
    * 2.06 m to NW / SW persons → both inside LDS range from t=0.
    * 2.50 m to centre person   → already in detector range.
    * 5.50 m to NE / SE persons → outside LDS range, will be
      observed once the bot translates toward them. This is the
      desired exploration progression.
-->
<sdf version="1.6">
  <world name="warehouse_models_person">

    <!-- Recording view: top-down user camera at load time. The same pose
         lives in docker/gazebo_gui.ini's [camera] section, but Gazebo
         Classic 11 ignores gui.ini camera poses (verified in R02a) while
         it does honor this <gui> block — so demo recordings no longer
         need the view set by hand. -->
    <gui>
      <camera name="user_camera">
        <pose>0.0 -1.2 10.5 0 1.35 1.5708</pose>
      </camera>
    </gui>

    <!-- Standard lighting -->
    <include>
      <uri>model://sun</uri>
    </include>

    <!-- Infinite ground plane -->
    <include>
      <uri>model://ground_plane</uri>
    </include>

    <!-- Physics: stable real-time factor -->
    <physics type="ode">
      <real_time_factor>1.0</real_time_factor>
      <max_step_size>0.001</max_step_size>
    </physics>

    <!-- Scene ambient -->
    <scene>
      <ambient>0.4 0.4 0.4 1</ambient>
      <background>0.7 0.75 0.8 1</background>
    </scene>

    <!-- ================================================================
         WALLS — enclosed 6 m × 6 m room.
         Inner clearance: X in [-3, 3], Y in [-3, 3].
         Thickness 0.2 m, height 2.5 m.
         ================================================================ -->

    <model name="wall_east">
      <static>true</static>
      <pose>3.1 0 1.25 0 0 0</pose>
      <link name="link">
        <collision name="collision">
          <geometry><box><size>0.2 6.4 2.5</size></box></geometry>
        </collision>
        <visual name="visual">
          <geometry><box><size>0.2 6.4 2.5</size></box></geometry>
          <material>
            <ambient>0.55 0.55 0.58 1</ambient>
            <diffuse>0.65 0.65 0.7 1</diffuse>
          </material>
        </visual>
      </link>
    </model>

    <model name="wall_west">
      <static>true</static>
      <pose>-3.1 0 1.25 0 0 0</pose>
      <link name="link">
        <collision name="collision">
          <geometry><box><size>0.2 6.4 2.5</size></box></geometry>
        </collision>
        <visual name="visual">
          <geometry><box><size>0.2 6.4 2.5</size></box></geometry>
          <material>
            <ambient>0.55 0.55 0.58 1</ambient>
            <diffuse>0.65 0.65 0.7 1</diffuse>
          </material>
        </visual>
      </link>
    </model>

    <model name="wall_north">
      <static>true</static>
      <pose>0 3.1 1.25 0 0 0</pose>
      <link name="link">
        <collision name="collision">
          <geometry><box><size>6.4 0.2 2.5</size></box></geometry>
        </collision>
        <visual name="visual">
          <geometry><box><size>6.4 0.2 2.5</size></box></geometry>
          <material>
            <ambient>0.5 0.52 0.55 1</ambient>
            <diffuse>0.6 0.62 0.65 1</diffuse>
          </material>
        </visual>
      </link>
    </model>

    <model name="wall_south">
      <static>true</static>
      <pose>0 -3.1 1.25 0 0 0</pose>
      <link name="link">
        <collision name="collision">
          <geometry><box><size>6.4 0.2 2.5</size></box></geometry>
        </collision>
        <visual name="visual">
          <geometry><box><size>6.4 0.2 2.5</size></box></geometry>
          <material>
            <ambient>0.5 0.52 0.55 1</ambient>
            <diffuse>0.6 0.62 0.65 1</diffuse>
          </material>
        </visual>
      </link>
    </model>

    <!-- ================================================================
         PERSON FIGURES — five instances of person_standing.
         Yaw points each corner-person toward the room centre (0, 0)
         so the figure is detectable from any approach angle.
         ================================================================ -->

    <!-- NE corner: blue shirt (R04 language-guided search demo; variants
         generated by scripts/make_person_variants.py) -->
    <include>
      <uri>model://person_blue_shirt</uri>
      <name>person_corner_ne</name>
      <pose>2.0 2.0 0 0 0 -2.3562</pose>
    </include>

    <!-- NW area: plain (white shirt). The green-shirt variant was
         first placed here (R04) but corner placement starved the search:
         exploration rarely faces a corner from <2.5 m (the memory range
         gate), so promotion never reached n=5. Green now lives at the
         centre. R05: pulled in from the (-2, 2) corner — the jersey
         barrier along the west wall spans y ±2.03 at x ≤ -2.15, which
         overlaps the old corner spots. -->
    <include>
      <uri>model://person_standing</uri>
      <name>person_corner_nw</name>
      <pose>-1.2 1.8 0 0 0 -0.7854</pose>
    </include>

    <!-- SW area: plain (white shirt). R05: moved with person_corner_nw. -->
    <include>
      <uri>model://person_standing</uri>
      <name>person_corner_sw</name>
      <pose>-1.2 -1.8 0 0 0 0.7854</pose>
    </include>

    <!-- SE corner: red shirt -->
    <include>
      <uri>model://person_red_shirt</uri>
      <name>person_corner_se</name>
      <pose>2.0 -2.0 0 0 0 2.3562</pose>
    </include>

    <!-- Centre: green shirt (R04 search-demo primary target — the centre
         is passed at close range on every exploration run, so the
         "person wearing a green shirt" search promotes reliably). -->
    <include>
      <uri>model://person_green_shirt</uri>
      <name>person_centre</name>
      <pose>0.0 0.0 0 0 0 0</pose>
    </include>

    <!-- ================================================================
         OPEN-VOCABULARY TARGETS (Phase 2b) — three objects that are NOT
         in the YOLO/COCO 80-class list, for the LocateAnything detect
         vocabulary. Placed at the mid-wall points (N/S/E; W stays clear
         for the default spawn at (-1.5, 0)), ≥ 2.0 m centre-to-centre
         from every person so the semantic memory merge radius (1.5 m)
         cannot fuse them. All three are solid at the LDS-01 scan height
         (~0.17 m), so the map-memory island check has real occupancy to
         snap to. `<static>true</static>` overrides the trash can's and
         cone's dynamic SDF so robot bumps cannot move them (the person
         models are already static).
         ================================================================ -->

    <!-- North wall: bookshelf, open side facing the room centre (its
         open side faces local -y at yaw 0). Occupies y ∈ [2.2, 2.61]. -->
    <include>
      <uri>model://bookshelf</uri>
      <name>bookshelf_north</name>
      <static>true</static>
      <pose>0.0 2.6 0 0 0 0</pose>
    </include>

    <!-- South wall: FIRST 2015 trash can (green, ~0.58 m dia). -->
    <include>
      <uri>model://first_2015_trash_can</uri>
      <name>trash_can_south</name>
      <static>true</static>
      <pose>0.0 -2.3 0 0 0 0</pose>
    </include>

    <!-- East wall: construction cone (orange). -->
    <include>
      <uri>model://construction_cone</uri>
      <name>cone_east</name>
      <static>true</static>
      <pose>2.4 0.0 0 0 0 0</pose>
    </include>

    <!-- West wall: jersey barrier (R05, registry class "traffic
         barrier"). The model is 4.07 x 0.81 m, so along the west wall
         (yaw 90°) it spans y in [-2.03, 2.03] at x in [-2.96, -2.15];
         the two white-shirt persons were pulled off the west corners to
         make room (see above). Baked into the sim image from osrf
         gazebo_models like the other four objects (docker/Dockerfile.sim). -->
    <include>
      <uri>model://jersey_barrier</uri>
      <name>barrier_west</name>
      <static>true</static>
      <pose>-2.55 0.0 0 0 0 1.5708</pose>
    </include>

  </world>
</sdf>
